<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Apple Privacy Manifest — REQUIRED for App Store submission since 2024-05-01.

  Two independent things live here:
  1. NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes — "required-reason" APIs the app's own code and the
     bundled Capacitor plugins call. These MUST be declared with an approved reason
     code or the build is rejected at upload with an automated email.
       • UserDefaults (CA92.1): Capacitor core + @capacitor/app / status-bar /
         splash-screen / local-notifications read/write the app's OWN UserDefaults.
       • File timestamp (C617.1): WKWebView local asset serving and file access
         inside the app container read file modification times.
       • Disk space (E174.1): WKWebView cache/file writes check available space.
     (System boot time is NOT used by first-party code or the wired plugins — omitted.)

  2. NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes — data the DEVELOPER collects. Companion is a client
     for the user's OWN self-hosted CI-Server: captured content is transmitted only to
     that user-controlled appliance, which the developer (Lifescope) has no access to.
     Under Apple's access-based definition of "collect" that argues for an empty list,
     and for most categories this manifest keeps it empty. NOTE: the App Store Connect
     "App Privacy" nutrition label is a SEPARATE artifact — declare the transmitted
     categories there honestly and explain the self-hosted architecture in review
     notes. If a Lifescope-hosted demo server is stood up for App Review, data flowing
     to THAT instance is developer-accessible and must be reflected in the label.

     PRECISE LOCATION IS DECLARED ANYWAY, and the asymmetry is deliberate. The
     device-native location source (see @ci/common utils/deviceSources/location.ts)
     transmits continuous position fixes off the device — the single most sensitive
     stream this app produces. DEVICE_DATA_COMPLIANCE.md §4 works the precedent:
     Immich, architecturally identical (user-owned server, no vendor cloud), still
     declares its payload as collected, and under-declaring is the failure mode that
     gets apps pulled while over-declaring costs a paragraph. The demo server
     DELIVERY.md §1b requires also makes the clean "developer cannot access it"
     reading untrue for the shipped binary. Linked: true (it rides the user's
     account); Tracking: false (no ad SDKs, no analytics, no third-party linkage);
     purpose: App Functionality only.
     Keep this in sync with the nutrition label — a manifest and a label pointing
     opposite directions is a reviewer question nobody wants to answer live.

  Tracking: the app shows no ads, runs no analytics SDKs, and links no third-party
  identifiers, so tracking is false with no tracking domains.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePreciseLocation</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryDiskSpace</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>E174.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
